{
  "teamId": 146,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Miami Marlins entered the season with cautious optimism, hoping to build on their surprising 2020 playoff appearance. But Opening Day delivered an immediate gut punch — Anthony Bass surrendered a three-run homer to Joey Wendle on April 2nd that erased an early lead, setting a tone of fragility in the bullpen that would haunt the club all year. April ended 11-15, a record that masked genuine competitive moments but also exposed a roster that was leaking runs at an alarming rate.\n\nMay and June brought the Marlins' most consistent stretches of meaningful play, with Jon Berti's clutch two-run single in late May and Garrett Cooper's timely home runs providing flashes of offensive life. Yet for every spark the lineup generated, the bullpen found ways to extinguish rallies and hand back leads. Yimi García and Dylan Floro in particular became recurring characters in the wrong kind of story, surrendering big hits in high-leverage moments. The team went 13-14 in May before a brutal 10-17 June confirmed that this squad lacked the pitching depth to compete in a tough division.\n\nJuly and August offered little reprieve. Jesús Aguilar provided genuine middle-of-the-order punch, clubbing a pair of meaningful home runs to keep individual games interesting, but the team's 10-16 July and 11-18 August told the real story. The Marlins were fading, and their postseason window had firmly closed. The trade deadline came and went — most notably with Starling Marte departing — and Miami entered its final act as a team playing for pride and development.\n\nSeptember became a showcase for the future rather than a push for relevance. Rookie Bryan De La Cruz announced himself with a series of clutch hits, including a walk-off single and a timely triple, while Jesús Sánchez and Lewin Díaz offered glimpses of what the next generation might bring. The Marlins finished 67-95, a significant step back from 2020, but the late-season emergence of young talent at least gave the fanbase something to hold onto heading into an uncertain offseason.",
